),\n$$\n\nwhat are the ranges of $x$ and $y?$" 13194139534655 true] [17592186087458 1100 "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n$x=t^2-6t, \\,\\,\\, y=t^4+4, \\quad t \\in (-\\infty, \\infty)$,\n\nwhat are the ranges of $x$ and $y$?" 13194139534655 false] [17592186087458 1145 "Let's consider each variable in turn.\n\n- To find the range of $x$, we can start by completing the square, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{rl}x & = {t}^{2} - 6 t \\\\  & = {( t - 3 )}^{2} - ( 3 {)}^{2} \\\\  & = {( t - 3 )}^{2} - 9\\end{array}\n$$\n\nSince $\\left(t-3\\right)^2 \\geq 0$, we can conclude that $x \\geq -9$.\n- To find the range of $y=t^4+4$, notice that $t^4 \\geq 0$, and consequently $y \\geq 4$.\n\nTherefore, the ranges are\n\n$$\nx \\geq -9\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ny \\geq 4\n$$" 13194139534655 false] [17592186087458 1145 "Let's consider each variable in turn.\n\n- To find the range of $x,$ we can start by completing the square, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={t}^{2}-6t \\\\  & ={(t-3)}^{2}-(3)^{2} \\\\  & ={(t-3)}^{2}-9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\left( t-3\\right)^2 \\geq 0,$ we can conclude that $x \\geq -9.$\n\n- To find the range of $y=t^4+4,$ notice that $t^4 \\geq 0,$ and consequently $y \\geq 4.$\n\nTherefore, the ranges are $x \\geq -9$ and $y \\geq 4.$" 13194139534655 true] [17592186087477 1100 "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx = 4\\cos\\theta, \\,\\,\\, y = 2\\sin\\theta, \\quad 0\\leq \\theta <     2\\pi,\n$$\n\nwhat are the ranges of $x$ and $y?$" 13194139534655 true] [17592186087477 1100 "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n$x = 4\\cos\\theta, \\,\\,\\, y = 2\\sin\\theta, \\quad 0\\leq \\theta < 2\\pi$,\nwhat are the ranges of $x$ and $y$?" 13194139534655 false] [17592186087477 1145 "Let's consider each variable in turn.\n\n- To find the range of $x$, notice that for $0\\leq\\theta < 2\\pi$, we have $-1 \\leq \\cos{\\theta} \\leq 1$. Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{rl}- 1 & \\le  \\cos   \\theta \\le  1 \\\\ - 4 & \\le  4 \\cos   \\theta \\le  4 \\\\ - 4 & \\le  x \\le  4.\\end{array}\n$$\n\n.\nSo, we can conclude that $-4 \\leq x \\leq 4$.\n- To find the range of $y$, notice that for $0\\leq\\theta < 2\\pi$, we have $-1 \\leq \\sin{\\theta} \\leq 1$. Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{rl}- 1 & \\le  \\sin   \\theta \\le  1 \\\\ - 2 & \\le  2 \\sin   \\theta \\le  2 \\\\ - 2 & \\le  y \\le  2.\\end{array}\n$$\n\n.\nSo, we can conclude that $-2 \\leq y \\leq 2$.\n\nTherefore, the ranges are\n\n$$\n-4 \\leq x \\leq 4\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n-2 \\leq y \\leq 2\n$$" 13194139534655 false] [17592186087477 1145 "Let's consider each variable in turn.\n\n- To find the range of $x,$ notice that for $0\\leq\\theta <  2\\pi,$ we have $-1 \\leq \\cos{\\theta} \\leq 1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ -4 & \\le 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le 4 \\\\ -4 & \\le x\\le 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can conclude that $-4 \\leq x \\leq 4.$\n\n- To find the range of $y,$ notice that for $0\\leq\\theta <  2\\pi,$ we have $-1 \\leq \\sin{\\theta} \\leq 1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ -2 & \\le 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le 2 \\\\ -2 & \\le y\\le 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can conclude that $-2 \\leq y \\leq 2.$\n\nTherefore, the ranges are $-4 \\leq x \\leq 4$ and $-2 \\leq y \\leq 2.$" 13194139534655 true] [17592186087654 1020 #edb/ref 17592186301646 13194139534655 true] [17592186087654 1020 #edb/ref 17592186301653 13194139534655 true] [17592186087658 1020 #edb/ref 17592186301626 13194139534655 true] [17592186087658 1020 #edb/ref 17592186301639 13194139534655 true] [17592186087659 1020 #edb/ref 17592186301619 13194139534655 true] [17592186087659 1020 #edb/ref 17592186301715 13194139534655 true] [17592186087673 1020 #edb/ref 17592186301661 13194139534655 true] [17592186087673 1020 #edb/ref 17592186301674 13194139534655 true] [17592186087673 1020 #edb/ref 17592186301689 